tegorical variable: blood type. We summarized this variable above, as we summarized single categorical variables in the EDA section, by listing what values the variable takes and how often it takes them. In EDA we used percentages, and here we’re using probabilities, but the two convey the same information. In the EDA section, we learned that a pie chart provides an appropriate display when a single categorical variable is involved, and similarly we can use it here (using percentages instead of probabilities):","position":{"start":{"line":46,"column":1},"end":{"line":46,"column":1}},"key":"eIRZ5CeDef"}],"key":"sXI6trtBDW"},{"type":"container","kind":"figure","children":[{"type":"image","url":"/textbook/build/m09-blood-pie-a3a51a307897c96aeee03813dc6d75c2.svg","alt":"A pie chart titled Blood Types in the United States. Type O takes up 44% of the pie, A takes 42%, B takes 10%, and AB takes 4%.","key":"MgkMsZuWi8","urlSource":"images/gen/m09-blood-pie.svg"}],"enumerator":"1","key":"DD12K3GraB"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":52,"column":1},"end":{"line":52,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Even though what we’re doing here is indeed similar to what we’ve done in the EDA section, there is a subtle but important difference between the underlying situations in this section and the ones in the Exploratory Data Analysis section. In EDA, we summarized data that were obtained from a ","position":{"start":{"line":52,"column":1},"end":{"line":52,"column":1}},"key":"p5LnF8LcVK"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":52,"column":1},"end":{"line":52,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"sample","position":{"start":{"line":52,"column":1},"end":{"line":52,"column":1}},"key":"BO3LgoDo3A"}],"key":"Vy5OGuYJk5"},{"type":"text","value":" of individuals for whom values of the variable of interest were recorded. Here, when we present the frequency, or probability, of each blood type, we have in mind the entire ","position":{"start":{"line